<DocsLayout title="Security" description="Understand signing, dependency, privacy, and trust boundaries before using scmle in a release workflow." current="security">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Trust boundaries</p>
  <h1>Know what is<br /><em>trusted.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">Security here means more than a clean dependency scan. It means knowing which bytes, identities, keys, and operators a claim depends on—and refusing to turn missing evidence into a green status.</p>

  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Local-first is not automatically safe.</strong> A local filesystem, signing key, webhook, or plugin still has an owner and a threat model. Put the package behind the controls your deployment requires.</p></div>

  <h2>Trust boundaries in one run</h2>
  <div class="diagram">
    <div class="diagram-caption"><span>Trust map</span><strong>identity is checked at every hand-off</strong></div>
    <div class="flow-row">
      <div class="flow-node active"><span>01 / INPUT</span><b>Source bytes</b><small>snapshot + digest</small></div><i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node"><span>02 / BUILD</span><b>Run evidence</b><small>curation + lineage</small></div><i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node"><span>03 / SHIP</span><b>Signed bundle</b><small>manifest + payload</small></div><i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node active"><span>04 / SERVE</span><b>Inference</b><small>exact digest</small></div>
    </div>
    <div class="evidence-rail"><span>operator evidence</span><i></i><span>review + policy</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Bundle verification is mandatory</h2>
  <p>A bundle load verifies the manifest signature, model payload digest, model digest, and fitted type before deserialization. Treat <code>trusted=True</code> as an explicit executable-code boundary, not as a convenience switch.</p>
  <pre><code>{`from scmle import ModelBundle

bundle = ModelBundle.load(
    "runs/run-42/model",
    trusted=True,
    signing_key=release_key,
)
# Verify the exact bundle digest before promotion or serving.`}</code></pre>
  <p>The key shown above must come from a deployment secret or approved key service. Do not commit it, print it into logs, or use one shared development key as release identity.</p>

  <h2>Key identity and emergency rotation</h2>
  <p>Bundle rotation changes the signature, but release operations also need to know which signer was allowed to create it. The metadata-only <code>SigningKeyRegistry</code> records key identity, owner, expiry, retirement, replacement, and revocation without storing the secret. Keep the secret in the deployment secret manager.</p>
  <pre><code>{`registry.sign(payload, key_id, secret, signed_at)
registry.retire(key_id, changed_at, "scheduled rotation", replacement_key_id)
registry.revoke(key_id, changed_at, "credential compromise")

# Retired keys verify old evidence only when explicitly requested.
registry.verify(payload, envelope, secrets, observed_at, historical=True)`}</code></pre>
  <p>Retirement stops new signatures while preserving auditable historical proof. Revocation is immediate, including historical verification. That is the intended emergency boundary: rotate forward without silently accepting evidence from a compromised signer.</p>

  <h2>Data and dependency controls</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Keep raw source bytes and derived evidence separate; redact PII in logs and sidecars according to the retention policy.</li>
    <li>Review image EXIF, document provenance, source URIs, and label fields before distribution.</li>
    <li>Pin CI actions, audit runtime and site dependencies, and retain the SBOM and license inventory with the release evidence.</li>
    <li>Keep optional extras outside the supported claim until their licenses, vulnerabilities, and resource assumptions are audited.</li>
    <li>Use HTTPS for alert webhooks. Local plaintext is allowed only in an explicit localhost test.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What this page does not promise</h2>
  <p>A valid local signature does not prove that the secret manager enforced custody, that an operator performed the rotation, or that an independent reviewer approved the release. A dependency audit does not prove that a connector, plugin, container, or host is safe. Record those controls in the release decision instead of inferring them from a passing test.</p>
  <p>The control behind each risk, the exact limits of data custody and operator guidance are on <a href={`${base}/docs/threat-model/`}>Threat model & controls</a>; caller identity, scopes and tenant rules are on <a href={`${base}/docs/tenant-isolation/`}>Tenant isolation</a>.</p>
  <p>For the operational response when a verification fails, go to <a href={`${base}/docs/recovery/`}>Recovery</a>. For the claims a reviewer must recompute, go to <a href={`${base}/docs/evidence/`}>Evidence & gates</a>.</p>

  <p class="meta">Security claims are scoped to the evidence that proves them · 0.1 beta</p>
</DocsLayout>
